1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,000
01 / Choose where AI runs
Native MLX classifies on this Mac. A separate local Ollama model extracts details. Cloud processing requires an explicit choice.

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:40,000
02 / Write naturally
A fictional note: “I want to read Sapiens.” The challenge is to identify both the subject and the action, without recording a completed activity.

3
00:00:40,000 --> 00:01:00,000
03 / Run the fine-tuned adapter
This run used reviewed730: BOOK / wishlist. The runtime adapter hash matches the public release. Processing waits are omitted; this is not a latency benchmark.

4
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:20,000
04 / Review the interpretation
The actual app proposes Book, Sapiens, Something I want to do. Classification and extraction remain fallible. The user reviews the details before Save.

5
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:40,000
05 / Confirm before writing
Only explicit Save writes the reviewed record to this browser’s vault. The app shows a durable save confirmation. No cloud AI was selected for this typed flow.

6
00:01:40,000 --> 00:02:00,000
06 / Return to your library
Sapiens appears as a Book / Wishlist item and survived reload. Experimental model: 67.0% to 76.5% joint on 200 notes, below the 81.0% majority baseline. Links and evidence accompany this demo.
